alan turing is someone who spent a lot  of time  thinking about the mystery of  consciousness  and what it means to be alive his work  in computing  also tackled these questions and with  his ideas about the turing machine and  the tiering test  he no doubt brushed up against the  questions of  whether a computer could be considered  to be alive or conscious  and where a classification would leave  the field of artificial  intelligence as a boy alan turing read a  book  called natural wonders by edwin tenny  brewster  and he later told his mum that it was  this book that opened his heart  and mind to science the book explores  lots of wonderful ideas around the  science of life  including questioning aliveness and a  chapter  on where we do our thinking while doing  research for this video i  enjoyed this book so much that i wanted  to read it out to you  and so i've started a podcast doing just  that  the first episode about how the chicken  gets inside the egg  is up now for everyone and the rest of  the episodes will be available  through my patreon for now though let's  have a look at the chapter  called how much of us is alive  the author talks about how much of a  tree  could be considered to be alive the bark  is dead  and the wood is dead between the two is  a  thin layer which is alive the green  leaves are alive  and the twigs but at least half of  every living tree is already dead  the blood we say is the life and yet  the blood itself is dead the watery part  is just soup water and salt and fat and  jelly  the minute coin-like red blood  corpuscles  carry the oxygen of the air from the  lungs all over the body  but there are similar oxygen carriers  likewise dead  in bottles in the drugstore maybe this  question of whether our blood is  considered alive or dead  could be better left to philosophers but  this is the view that the author holds  and he describes us as being made of  living bricks but set in a dead  mortar perhaps it is the incompleteness  of this explanation that inspired alan  turing  to want to explore how to get from  a series of living bricks to  intelligence  and turing did have a lifelong passion  for  biology i'll put on screen some pictures  from some of his less known work on the  topic of  morphogenesis and trying to understand  where the patterns and living things  whether it's  in the fur of animals and the patterns  and the colors  all the way down to the way the seeds  arrange themselves in a flower head  turing was trying to understand how this  could happen  from kind of a mathematical perspective  and these ideas are also particular  favorites of mine i've always been  really fascinated by this  interface of biology and maths and  finding patterns in nature so  i really love having a look at some of  this work  questioning the nature of aliveness  is also something that influenced the  way that  alan turing approached childhood grief  an important part of the story of alan  turing's life  is that of his childhood best friend and  possibly first love christopher morkham  alan and christopher would spend a lot  of time together at the  sherbourne school library where they  would discuss their ideas in mathematics  and science  christopher was a year older than ellen  and also a very gifted mathematician  he had won a scholarship to study at  cambridge university  but he died in 1930 at age 18  before he was ever able to take up his  place  he died from bovine tuberculosis which  came from drinking infected cow's milk  and the loss was really difficult for  touring  he stayed close with christopher's  mother and wrote her a letter  all about his thoughts on the spirit in  which he says that  the body provides something for the  spirit  to look after and use it sounds like  this was his way of  wrapping his head around the loss and  also comforting her  tearing also resolved to work extra hard  going forward  to make up for the great potential that  had been lost  christopher's mum created a science  prize at the school  to help remember christopher bai and the  very first winner of that prize  was alan turing and as part of his prize  he got to select a book the one he chose  was called  mathematical recreations and essays  the last chapter of this book was about  cryptographs and  ciphers and it was quite possibly the  first  place where turing encountered those  ideas  eight years later he would be working at  the british code breaking organization  and helping to develop solutions to  decrypting  enigma machine messages now some of  these solutions that he was helping to  develop  were first worked out by polish code  breakers  the chapter starts by giving some  definitions of a cryptograph and a  cipher  it says here that a cryptograph may be  defined as a manner of writing  in which the letters or symbols employed  are used in their normal sense  but are so arranged that the  communication is intelligible  only to those possessing the key a  simple example  is a communication in which every word  is spelt backwards  a cipher may be defined as a manner of  writing  by characters arbitrarily invented or by  an arbitrary  use of letters words or characters  in any other than their ordinary sense  intelligible only to those possessing  the key  a simple example is when each letter is  replaced by the one that  immediately follows it in the natural  order of the alphabet  a being replaced by b b by c  and so on finally z by a  in a communication cipher symbols may be  arranged cryptographically  and thus further hinder a reading of the  message  so the message above as a cryptographic  cipher  would be this quite confusing message  down here  but of course these messages get much  more complicated than these examples  later in the chapter the author goes on  to talk about cipher machines  he says that there's a risk of some  instrument  when set falling into unauthorized hands  and this must be taken into account  since equally good ciphers can be  constructed without the use of a  mechanical device  i do not think their employment can be  recommended  now after this was written cipher  machines  would go on to become the norm and alan  turing who read this book  would go on to attack the enigma machine  which  is an example of one so perhaps not  everything in this book is  totally up to date but both of these  books i've mentioned  so far are now in the public domain so  you can read them yourself for free  and there'll be links in the description  to do that  of course there are plenty of other  books and people  who inspired alan turing throughout his  life and  in his childhood and we can see another  resource here which is  the complete list of library books that  alan borrowed while he was a student at  the sherbourne school as mentioned in my  previous video  he read a lot of books about math  science  physics and astronomy and not so many  fiction books  and his english teacher tried to  encourage him to read more of those  he did read the alice in wonderland  books which did have a bit of a  mathematical bend to them  but i find looking at this list of books  that he read as a student  to be pretty interesting it would seem  to me that even when he was young  he knew what he was interested in and he  pursued those things he was interested  in  and eventually found success in them  thanks as always to my patreon  supporters  and a special shout out to today's  patreon cat of the day  cheeto thanks for watching and i hope to  see you next time
 

